WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:01.501
So a lot

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:01.935
of people might

00:00:01.935 --> 00:00:02.569
not know

00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:03.470
that your dad

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:06.006
was actually in the news biz. Yeah.

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:07.140
I mean, your mom was a nurse,

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.441
but your dad was in the news business.

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:10.643
And I'm wondering how much you think

00:00:10.643 --> 00:00:12.512
that might have had an effect on the fact

00:00:12.512 --> 00:00:14.748
that you do very news oriented

00:00:14.748 --> 00:00:16.416
content tremendously.

00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:17.817
I think it was a great advantage,

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:18.718
first of all.

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:20.120
He was also funny,

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.287
and you'll find this a lot

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:22.956
with comedians.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.425
That comedy gene sort of

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:28.395
just aged through a generation.

00:00:28.428 --> 00:00:29.763
I grew up in a house

00:00:29.763 --> 00:00:32.098
that was interested in the news.

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:33.633
My parents talked about the news.

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:36.136
I was aware of it went away.

00:00:36.136 --> 00:00:37.804
I don't think most kids were.

00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:39.239
So those are my influences.

00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:40.940
Comedy and news.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.509
And then what did I do?

00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:44.077
I wound up doing a show

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:45.478
that is a comedy show

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:47.714
that uses the news as fodder

00:00:48.248 --> 00:00:50.750
and I wish my father

00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:51.718
would have lived to see it,

00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:52.819
but he passed away early.

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:55.422
Nobody saw me like do Johnny Carson,

00:00:55.422 --> 00:00:57.090
but you didn't see Politically Incorrect.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.024
What's your response

00:00:58.024 --> 00:00:59.259
when people tell you that

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:00.593
and you must hear this a lot,

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:02.495
that they get

00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:02.896
that you're

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:04.297
one of their primary sources

00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:05.632
of information?

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:07.734
Flattered and as it should be.

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:10.770
My response?

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:13.239
I feel like

00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:15.108
people are looking for something

00:01:15.108 --> 00:01:17.710
that is not slanted.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.412
I mean, I certainly have lost fans

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:20.113
over the years

00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:22.015
because I don't say the things

00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:23.316
that pleased people all the time.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:24.818
But that's my ultimate bond

00:01:24.818 --> 00:01:26.219
with the audience.

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:27.420
When I lose people,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:27.720
it's like,

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:28.388
okay, well,

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:30.190
you were never meant to be with me

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:31.291
in the first place.

00:01:31.291 --> 00:01:33.393
Yeah, we can't have a relationship.

00:01:33.393 --> 00:01:34.627
There are a whole lot of people

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:36.329
who are just conditioned

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:38.131
to only hearing, only wanting

00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:39.799
to hear one side of the story. Correct.

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:41.768
They do not want to hear the other side.

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:43.169
I mean, that's it's so ironic.

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:43.770
When I started

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:44.771
on Politically Incorrect

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:47.574
30 years ago, 1993,

00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:48.641
everybody said,

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:48.975
you know,

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:50.543
you can't really do a show like this

00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:51.778
because you're giving your opinions.

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:53.813
That's not what talk show hosts do.

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:56.015
They don't and can't give their opinions.

00:01:56.015 --> 00:01:57.984
You'll alienate half the crowd.

00:01:57.984 --> 00:01:59.419
That's not what Johnny Carson did.

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:00.887
That's not what David Letterman did.

00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:03.089
That's not what Jay Leno did.

00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:04.457
And I said, Well, let's give it a try.

00:02:05.558 --> 00:02:06.126
Maybe the

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:07.727
people are a little more sophisticated

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:09.329
than you're giving them credit for,

00:02:09.329 --> 00:02:11.664
and they can actually take it that

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:13.066
a guy

00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:14.100
who's on TV,

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.301
they don't always agree with him,

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:16.436
but they still like him.

00:02:16.436 --> 00:02:17.570
Just like in life,

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:18.304
you don't always agree

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:20.373
with your friends on everything.

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:22.542
So it did turn out that it was okay

00:02:22.542 --> 00:02:24.677
because here I have 30 years later

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:25.645
still on.

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:27.380
But I get every year

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:28.781
when we are looking for

00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:30.817
maybe we want new writers on our show.

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:32.585
I love the writers I have.

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:33.353
But you know what?

00:02:33.353 --> 00:02:34.921
I'm running a business here, right?

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:35.522
I'm sorry.

00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:36.389
It's it's brutal.

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:37.957
But if I can find somebody better,

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:38.791
that's what I'm doing.

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:40.727
I always serve the show first.

00:02:40.727 --> 00:02:41.227
So every year

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:43.563
I read these packets of proposed writers,

00:02:43.563 --> 00:02:44.697
and I read them this year,

00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:45.498
as I do every year.

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:47.567
And it's just stunning

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.569
how uniform their

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:51.571
their point of view are.

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:52.238
And then always it

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:54.007
hasn't always been that exact.

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:54.774
I don't remember,

00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:56.009
but I don't think it was ever quite

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:56.776
this bad.

00:02:56.776 --> 00:02:58.845
It's the exact same point of view

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:01.080
on every single issue,

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.582
and it's very predictable.

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:03.583
I have a relationship

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:04.050
with people

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.451
who want to hear

00:03:05.451 --> 00:03:06.686
what I think is the truth,

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:08.688
and I'm going to present both sides.

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:11.324
And they may not be fair and balanced.

00:03:11.324 --> 00:03:13.293
They may not be equal weight

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:14.227
put to each side

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:15.595
because that's not what the truth is.

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:17.263
The truth isn't always 5050.

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:17.797
Right.

00:03:18.965 --> 00:03:19.999
So

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:21.668
I live with that.

00:03:21.668 --> 00:03:23.469
But it's, it's it's

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:24.771
it gets more difficult

00:03:24.771 --> 00:03:26.439
because we're so tribal now.

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:27.273
So it's interesting about

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:29.175
this is because this comes after,

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:32.312
you know, five, six years of Trump

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:33.479
where it's not like

00:03:33.479 --> 00:03:35.481
you pull punches against him at all.

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:39.018
No, nobody was harder on that man,

00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:40.086
I don't think.

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:42.388
And honestly, nobody was more

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:43.890
had their hair on fire

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.425
about what was going to happen

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:47.827
as far as him not believing in democracy

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:48.828
and not wanting to leave.

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:50.597
I don't think he's leaving

00:03:50.597 --> 00:03:53.967
if even if he loses the election in 2020,

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.903
he could lose by a landslide in 2020.

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:58.371
And I still think he would say

00:03:58.371 --> 00:04:01.007
it's rigged. Fake News Deep State.

00:04:01.074 --> 00:04:03.142
If Trump loses the election in November,

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:05.345
he's not going to leave.

00:04:05.345 --> 00:04:07.614
You were predicting that in 2018?

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:09.315
Oh, 16, 16 years

00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:10.450
before he was even president.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:11.451
He's not going to leave.

00:04:11.451 --> 00:04:12.752
Never going to leave. Right.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.523
And what makes you think that

00:04:17.523 --> 00:04:18.992
he's seen this man?

00:04:18.992 --> 00:04:20.460
He's everything

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:21.427
wrong with a human

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:22.996
being stuffed into one man.

00:04:22.996 --> 00:04:24.030
I mean,

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.065
how could he ever do anything different?

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:27.400
He is incapable,

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.736
I think, of ever conceding defeat.

00:04:29.736 --> 00:04:31.237
And I've never seen him do it.

00:04:31.237 --> 00:04:32.305
So why would I think

00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:34.040
he would be gracious ever

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:36.943
in that situation or say he ever lost

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:38.444
and he said it?

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:40.613
I mean, even in 2016 when he was running,

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.149
they would ask him point blank,

00:04:43.149 --> 00:04:44.284
would you?

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:45.918
Well, it's

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:47.153
you know, he's basically saying,

00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:48.354
if we win, yes,

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:49.389
then it's a fair election.

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:50.857
If we don't, it was rigged.

00:04:50.857 --> 00:04:51.791
He would never actually

00:04:51.791 --> 00:04:53.259
just come out and say, yes, of course,

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:55.061
this is America. This is how we do it.

00:04:55.061 --> 00:04:56.462
This is the jewel in our crown

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:57.096
in this country

00:04:57.096 --> 00:04:59.065
with the peaceful transfer of power.

00:04:59.065 --> 00:04:59.999
Nothing like that.

00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:01.200
You've been the great predictor

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:01.868
of Donald Trump.

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:02.669
You thought he could win.

00:05:02.669 --> 00:05:03.936
You thought he wasn't going to leave

00:05:03.936 --> 00:05:06.339
peacefully. He's running.

00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:06.706
Oh, yeah.

00:05:06.706 --> 00:05:07.006
Do you think

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:08.041
he's going to get the nomination?

00:05:08.041 --> 00:05:09.509
And do you think he can beat Joe Biden?

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:11.644
Both.

00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:12.245
But both

00:05:12.245 --> 00:05:13.546
you think affirmatively are both.

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:15.748
And you think they could happen?

00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:16.983
Well, first of all, it could

00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:19.585
it might not be Joe Biden.

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:22.088
I think if it's Biden against Trump,

00:05:22.088 --> 00:05:23.823
Biden will win.

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:24.757
I do

00:05:25.558 --> 00:05:27.026
not guarantee,

00:05:27.026 --> 00:05:28.695
but I think that's a really good bet.

00:05:28.695 --> 00:05:29.629
It was the first time

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:30.963
and I think it would be the second time.

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:32.999
Even more so even at age 82.

00:05:33.399 --> 00:05:34.934
Yes, absolutely.

00:05:34.934 --> 00:05:36.002
Well, first of all,

00:05:36.002 --> 00:05:36.436
I think

00:05:36.436 --> 00:05:37.737
that's a big red herring

00:05:37.737 --> 00:05:40.173
that his age is such a factor.

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:42.709
You don't need to be

00:05:42.709 --> 00:05:45.678
young or spry to be president.

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:46.612
Yes, it's

00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:48.781
very helpful to run for president.

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:50.817
You need to be energetic and

00:05:50.817 --> 00:05:52.385
look sharp at the debate.

00:05:52.385 --> 00:05:54.120
He could do the job of President

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:54.921
perfectly fine.

00:05:54.921 --> 00:05:55.922
And I think he's done

00:05:55.922 --> 00:05:57.457
the job perfectly fine.

00:05:57.457 --> 00:05:58.891
Do I love everything he has done?

00:05:58.891 --> 00:05:59.792
Absolutely not.

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:01.194
But he's president.

00:06:01.194 --> 00:06:02.829
That goes with the territory.

00:06:02.829 --> 00:06:05.298
But generally he's restored normality.

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:06.766
He's got some things

00:06:06.766 --> 00:06:07.500
passed that

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:08.868
nobody thought he could get through this

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:10.436
divided Congress.

00:06:10.436 --> 00:06:11.170
I've got it.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:12.405
Got out of Afghanistan.

00:06:12.405 --> 00:06:13.873
Did he stick the landing on that one?

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:15.508
No, but at least he did it.

00:06:15.508 --> 00:06:18.378
I mean, Ukraine, I think he's done fine.

00:06:19.412 --> 00:06:21.447
And every other country in the world

00:06:21.447 --> 00:06:22.315
seems to have gotten

00:06:22.315 --> 00:06:25.818
this idea about the elderly, their wives.

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:27.487
That's what you need.

00:06:27.487 --> 00:06:30.289
I don't mind at all that he's 82 or 84.

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:32.158
86. Yes.

00:06:32.158 --> 00:06:33.459
If he loses his marbles.

00:06:33.459 --> 00:06:34.994
But there are plenty of 86 year

00:06:34.994 --> 00:06:35.395
old people

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:37.530
who have not lost their marbles

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:40.032
and people used to get that.

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:42.268
We have an important decision to make.

00:06:42.268 --> 00:06:44.337
Go to the elders.

00:06:44.337 --> 00:06:45.271
They will tell you what

00:06:45.271 --> 00:06:47.240
the wise decision is and then you young

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:48.808
people will go and carry it out.

00:06:48.808 --> 00:06:49.509
He doesn't have to go

00:06:49.509 --> 00:06:51.210
and do every little thing.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:52.478
I mean, Reagan didn't do anything.

00:06:52.478 --> 00:06:54.447
He went to bed at 430.

00:06:54.447 --> 00:06:57.450
So that does not bother me at all.

00:06:57.784 --> 00:06:58.284
No, it's

00:06:58.284 --> 00:06:59.752
going to bother people in the country

00:06:59.752 --> 00:07:01.487
because when you run for president,

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:03.723
you get exposed. Your flaws.

00:07:03.723 --> 00:07:04.524
And yes,

00:07:04.657 --> 00:07:05.792
when you're in your eighties, he's

00:07:05.792 --> 00:07:06.392
going to stumble.

00:07:06.392 --> 00:07:07.593
He was always a gaffe machine

00:07:07.593 --> 00:07:09.095
to begin with. Yeah.

00:07:09.095 --> 00:07:11.230
So it could look bad.

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:13.065
But I think as if it's Trump

00:07:13.065 --> 00:07:19.305
against Biden, I think Biden will win.

00:07:19.305 --> 00:07:20.840
But what if it's not?

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:21.974
What if it's not Biden?

00:07:21.974 --> 00:07:22.708
I don't know.

00:07:22.708 --> 00:07:24.243
So you talk about the Democrats

00:07:24.243 --> 00:07:26.446
being so hemmed in by identity politics.

00:07:26.446 --> 00:07:28.648
The counterargument would be

00:07:28.648 --> 00:07:30.116
it's always been identity politics.

00:07:30.116 --> 00:07:31.451
It's just always been white people.

00:07:31.451 --> 00:07:33.753
So people like you and me didn't notice.

00:07:33.753 --> 00:07:34.420
And now it's

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:36.355
just an effort at inclusion,

00:07:36.355 --> 00:07:38.758
which I'm sure theoretically you support.

00:07:38.858 --> 00:07:41.561
Yeah, I support it, in fact.

00:07:42.762 --> 00:07:44.831
But I mean, the you know, the

00:07:46.032 --> 00:07:47.934
Democrats sometimes can take it

00:07:47.934 --> 00:07:49.001
too far or,

00:07:49.001 --> 00:07:49.235
you know,

00:07:49.235 --> 00:07:49.535
I would

00:07:49.535 --> 00:07:51.437
I would categorize liberal

00:07:51.437 --> 00:07:53.039
as different than woke.

00:07:53.039 --> 00:07:55.007
Woke, which started out as a good thing.

00:07:55.007 --> 00:07:56.542
Alert to injustice.

00:07:56.542 --> 00:07:57.677
Who could be against that?

00:07:57.677 --> 00:08:00.112
But it became sort of an eye roll because

00:08:00.746 --> 00:08:04.016
they love diversity, accept of ideas.

00:08:05.151 --> 00:08:07.053
And that's not really where we should be.

00:08:07.053 --> 00:08:08.354
I mean, they have a trail

00:08:08.354 --> 00:08:10.790
of very bad ideas, I would think.

00:08:11.491 --> 00:08:13.593
And wokeness.

00:08:13.593 --> 00:08:15.127
Like, how do you define wokeness?

00:08:15.127 --> 00:08:15.928
Because I hear people

00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:16.762
use the term all the time

00:08:16.762 --> 00:08:18.130
and it means something different

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:19.098
to everybody.

00:08:19.098 --> 00:08:19.532
Well, again,

00:08:19.532 --> 00:08:21.734
I think it's this collection of ideas

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:23.035
that are

00:08:23.035 --> 00:08:24.604
not building on liberalism,

00:08:24.604 --> 00:08:26.672
but very often undoing it.

00:08:26.672 --> 00:08:29.141
I mean, five years ago,

00:08:29.141 --> 00:08:30.076
Abraham Lincoln

00:08:30.076 --> 00:08:31.744
was not a controversial figure

00:08:31.744 --> 00:08:33.746
among liberals. We liked him.

00:08:35.081 --> 00:08:35.715
Now they

00:08:35.715 --> 00:08:36.182
take his

00:08:36.182 --> 00:08:36.782
name off

00:08:36.782 --> 00:08:39.218
schools and tear down his statues.

00:08:39.252 --> 00:08:39.652
Really?

00:08:39.652 --> 00:08:42.722
Lincoln isn't good enough for you?

00:08:42.889 --> 00:08:45.224
Five, ten years ago, I

00:08:46.325 --> 00:08:48.728
bedrock liberalism was.

00:08:49.128 --> 00:08:50.496
We are striving to be

00:08:50.496 --> 00:08:51.797
a colorblind society

00:08:51.797 --> 00:08:54.166
where we don't see race.

00:08:54.166 --> 00:08:55.001
Of course we see it,

00:08:55.001 --> 00:08:56.936
but it doesn't matter.

00:08:56.969 --> 00:08:58.871
That's not what woke is.

00:08:58.871 --> 00:09:00.473
Woke is something very different.

00:09:00.473 --> 00:09:01.974
It's it's identity politics.

00:09:01.974 --> 00:09:03.009
We see it all the time.

00:09:03.009 --> 00:09:05.011
It's always the most important thing.

00:09:05.011 --> 00:09:07.079
I don't think that's liberalism.

00:09:07.079 --> 00:09:08.080
I mean, I could mention

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:10.283
so many issues like that.

00:09:10.283 --> 00:09:12.385
I remember doing that

00:09:12.485 --> 00:09:13.986
show on HBO,

00:09:13.986 --> 00:09:15.721
Comic Relief for the Homeless.

00:09:15.721 --> 00:09:19.525
And the idea then again among liberals,

00:09:19.525 --> 00:09:20.626
I thought was,

00:09:20.626 --> 00:09:21.027
you know,

00:09:21.027 --> 00:09:22.295
for the sake of compassion,

00:09:22.295 --> 00:09:23.930
